# WebAssembly architecture configuration
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

config WASM
	bool
	select ARCH_IS_SET
	select ATOMIC_OPERATIONS_BUILTIN
	select USE_SWITCH
	select USE_SWITCH_SUPPORTED
	select ARCH_HAS_CUSTOM_BUSY_WAIT
	select ARCH_HAS_SEMIHOST
	select IRQ_OFFLOAD_NESTED if IRQ_OFFLOAD
	help
	  WebAssembly architecture. The kernel runs freestanding in a single
	  wasm32 linear memory; the host supplies devices as imported
	  functions rather than as memory-mapped registers.

if WASM

menu "WebAssembly Options"

config ARCH
	string
	default "wasm"

# Context switching runs on Binaryen's Asyncify transform for now. Everything
# goes through one internal interface so a stack-switching backend can replace
# it later without touching the rest of the arch.
config WASM_ASYNCIFY
	bool
	default y
	help
	  Use Asyncify to save and restore thread stacks.

config WASM_ASYNCIFY_BUFFER_SIZE
	int "Bytes of each thread stack reserved for the Asyncify buffer"
	default 8192 if MBEDTLS || NET_ZPERF
	default 4096
	help
	  Every thread stack object is split in two: the low part is the C
	  shadow stack, the high part is the Asyncify buffer that holds
	  unwound wasm frames.

	  Spike C measured the buffer needing roughly 88 bytes plus 32 per
	  frame live when the thread yields. Asyncify does not bounds-check
	  this buffer as it writes: overflowing it corrupts whatever follows.
	  The host notices once the thread has suspended and stops the run,
	  naming the buffer, but the damage is done by then, so the default is
	  deliberately generous.

	  A TLS handshake suspends from deeper in the stack than anything
	  else measured: tests/net/socket/tls needed 4160 bytes. So a build
	  with mbedTLS gets twice the usual, and so does one with zperf, whose
	  upload suspends from the shell thread, below a command handler, the
	  shell and the socket layer: a UDP upload needed 4240.

config WASM_STACK_HEADROOM
	int "Bytes of C stack each thread gets beyond the size it asked for"
	default 8192 if DEBUG_OPTIMIZATIONS || NO_OPTIMIZATIONS
	default 0
	help
	  Upstream sizes its thread stacks for native targets. Optimised,
	  wasm code needs no more than that, but built for debugging (-Og, as
	  CONFIG_DEBUG=y does) it keeps far more on the shadow stack.
	  smf_calculator, which asks for CONFIG_DEBUG, gives its own thread
	  1 KB. It ran off the bottom of that into the kernel objects, the log
	  core's buffer and the log sources' names linked below it: first the
	  run went silent, with main's timeout and the shell thread
	  overwritten; with 2048 more it trapped on a corrupted function
	  pointer; with 4096 more it ran, but printed its log source's name as
	  garbage. With 8192 more, and with 16384, an eleven-key session
	  printed the same, correctly.

	  Nothing guards the C stack (DESIGN.md D8), so too little shows up
	  later, somewhere else. The extra is reserved in every stack object,
	  above the size asked for, so the kernel's view of the stack is
	  unchanged, and only a debugging build pays for it.

config WASM_SAFEPOINTS
	bool "Check for pending interrupts at loop back-edges"
	default y
	help
	  Nothing preempts a running wasm function, so without this a thread
	  that never yields cannot be interrupted at all, and time slicing
	  does not work.

	  With it, a post-link pass inserts a call to z_wasm_safepoint at the
	  top of every loop body. The cost is one load and branch per
	  iteration of every loop in the image.

config WASM_SAFEPOINTS_PER_TICK
	int "Safepoints between progress reports to the host"
	default 20000
	depends on WASM_SAFEPOINTS
	help
	  Under virtual time the clock only advances when the kernel idles, so
	  a thread that spins without calling the kernel would freeze it. Every
	  this many safepoints, the guest gives the host a chance to advance
	  time and raise a deadline.

	  Lower is more responsive and slower; higher is the reverse. This is
	  the knob that decides how coarse preemption is for code that never
	  calls into the kernel.

config WASM_RUN_MAX_TIME_MS
	int "Guest time limit for `west build -t run`"
	default 60000
	help
	  Bounds a run started through the build system, which is also how
	  twister drives this board.

config WASM_RUN_REALTIME
	bool "Follow the wall clock for `west build -t run`"
	help
	  Off by default, so runs are reproducible.

config WASM_IRQ_LINES
	int "Number of host interrupt lines"
	default 16
	help
	  Size of the software ISR table. Interrupts arrive cooperatively: the
	  host sets a pending word in linear memory and the kernel notices it
	  at a safepoint.

# Picolibc's default is a malloc arena from the linker symbol _end to the end
# of RAM, and wasm-ld has no linker script to define _end. This is native_sim's
# answer to the same problem: a fixed arena in BSS. A build that chooses the
# minimal libc keeps its own default of none. Sourced before lib/libc, so this default is seen first.
config COMMON_LIBC_MALLOC_ARENA_SIZE
	default 16384 if !MINIMAL_LIBC

# PTHREAD_STACK_MIN is K_KERNEL_STACK_LEN(0), which here is what every stack
# reserves, the Asyncify buffer and any headroom, so the kernel's default of
# 1024 for a dynamically allocated stack makes pthread_create() refuse every
# thread. x86 has the same default for a similar reason. Kconfig cannot add,
# so the sums are spelled out.
config DYNAMIC_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
	default 16384 if WASM_ASYNCIFY_BUFFER_SIZE = 8192 && WASM_STACK_HEADROOM = 8192
	default 12288 if WASM_ASYNCIFY_BUFFER_SIZE = 4096 && WASM_STACK_HEADROOM = 8192
	default WASM_ASYNCIFY_BUFFER_SIZE

endmenu

endif # WASM

config WASM_INSPECT
	bool "Let the host ask about the kernel's threads"
	default y
	select THREAD_MONITOR
	select THREAD_NAME
	select THREAD_STACK_INFO
	help
	  Exports a walk of the kernel's thread list in a shape the host can
	  read, so a page can show which threads exist and which one holds the
	  CPU. The host learns no struct offsets: it asks and is answered.

	  The cost is the three options above, which the kernel would not
	  otherwise carry.

config WASM_INSPECT_STACK_SIZE
	int "Stack for the inspection walk"
	default 2048
	depends on WASM_INSPECT
	help
	  The walk runs on a stack of its own rather than spending the headroom
	  of whichever thread happens to be suspended when the host asks. The
	  host also borrows its top for the records the walk writes, 48 bytes
	  for each of up to 24 threads, so the walk's own stack is what is
	  left below them.
